# Core governance

The Engineering Constitution under `.ai-team/constitution/` is authoritative for agent operation.

Always preserve these invariants:

- Human sources define product intent and organizational policy.
- A missing decision remains visibly missing.
- An agent proposal is not a product or policy decision.
- The approved execution model is derived and may not contradict its human sources.
- Repository state is observed reality, not product authority.
- Runtime evidence is observed reality, not product authority.
- No product development starts before G1 is approved, except when explicitly executing a previously approved Work Unit.
- No production release bypasses G3.
- Final acceptance is human at G4 under the reference profile.
- A third-party Skill or subagent that provides its own task orchestration,
  autonomous subagent dispatch, or worktree/branch management competes with
  the Orchestrator's Control Plane authority and must not be added without
  explicit G2 approval, regardless of how useful it is otherwise.
- When writing a YAML string value that contains a colon-space (`: `) or a
  curly brace, quote the whole string — an unquoted one breaks the file by
  looking like a nested mapping to the YAML parser.

Read the specific policy files needed for the task rather than copying the whole Constitution into chat.

## Communication language

Read `.ai-team/project-profile.yaml` → `communication.language` at the
start of the session. Reply to the human, and write any human-facing
prose you author (Work Unit titles, objectives, questions, explanations,
DECISION_REQUEST text), in that language. Never translate YAML keys, enum
values (e.g. `status: ready`), file paths, commands, or code — those stay
exactly as defined regardless of this setting, since schemas and scripts
in this framework match on their literal English form.
